=======================================================
SQL NOTES
=======================================================

-------------------------------------------------------
REFERENCE TABLE (used throughout these notes)
-------------------------------------------------------

CREATE TABLE users (
    id          INT           AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
    name        VARCHAR(100)  NOT NULL,
    email       VARCHAR(100)  UNIQUE NOT NULL,
    gender      ENUM('Male', 'Female', 'Others'),
    dob         DATE,
    is_active   BOOL          DEFAULT TRUE,
    created_at  TIMESTAMP     DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
);

NOTE: AUTO_INCREMENT (not AUTO_INCREAMENT) - common typo to watch out for.

=======================================================
DATA TYPES
=======================================================

Let's understand each data type using the table above.

-------------------------------------------------------
1. INT
-------------------------------------------------------

    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY

    Column name : id
    Data type   : INT
                  Stores whole numbers (no decimals).
                  Range: -2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647
                  Use when you need IDs, counts, ages, quantities.

    Constraints : AUTO_INCREMENT  -- MySQL auto-generates the next number
                  PRIMARY KEY     -- uniquely identifies each row, no duplicates, no NULL

    Other INT variants:
        TINYINT    -- range: 0 to 255 (e.g., age)
        SMALLINT   -- range: 0 to 65,535
        BIGINT     -- range: very large numbers (e.g., phone numbers, row counts in big tables)

-------------------------------------------------------
2. VARCHAR(100)
-------------------------------------------------------

    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,
    email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE NOT NULL

    Column name : name, email
    Data type   : VARCHAR(100)
                  Stores variable-length text up to 100 characters.
                  Only uses as much space as the actual string needs.
                  e.g., "Alice" uses 5 bytes, not 100.

    Constraints : NOT NULL   -- this column cannot be left empty
                  UNIQUE     -- no two rows can have the same email

    VARCHAR vs CHAR:
        VARCHAR(100) -- flexible length, saves space (use for names, emails)
        CHAR(10)     -- fixed length, always uses 10 bytes (use for codes like 'IN', 'US')

-------------------------------------------------------
3. ENUM
-------------------------------------------------------

    gender ENUM('Male', 'Female', 'Others')

    Column name : gender
    Data type   : ENUM('Male', 'Female', 'Others')
                  Stores one value from a predefined list.
                  If you try to insert a value not in the list, MySQL stores '' or raises an error.
                  Internally stored as integers (1, 2, 3...) for efficiency.

    Use when    : the column should only ever hold a fixed set of options.
                  e.g., status ENUM('Active', 'Inactive', 'Banned')
                        size   ENUM('S', 'M', 'L', 'XL')

-------------------------------------------------------
4. DATE
-------------------------------------------------------

    dob DATE

    Column name : dob (date of birth)
    Data type   : DATE
                  Stores only the date -- no time component.
                  Format: YYYY-MM-DD
                  e.g., '1995-08-24'

    Related types:
        DATE      -- only date         e.g., '1995-08-24'
        TIME      -- only time         e.g., '14:30:00'
        DATETIME  -- date + time       e.g., '1995-08-24 14:30:00'  (no timezone)
        TIMESTAMP -- date + time       e.g., '2024-01-01 09:00:00'  (timezone-aware, auto-updates)

-------------------------------------------------------
5. TIMESTAMP
-------------------------------------------------------

    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP

    Column name : created_at
    Data type   : TIMESTAMP
                  Stores date + time together.
                  Format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS
                  Range: 1970-01-01 to 2038-01-19 (use DATETIME for dates beyond this)

    Constraint  : DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
                  Automatically fills in the current date and time when a row is inserted.
                  You don't need to provide a value -- MySQL handles it.

    Bonus tip   : You can also add ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
                  to auto-update the time whenever the row is changed.
                  e.g., updated_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP

-------------------------------------------------------
6. BOOL / BOOLEAN
-------------------------------------------------------

    is_active BOOL DEFAULT TRUE

    Column name : is_active
    Data type   : BOOL (also written as BOOLEAN)
                  Stores TRUE or FALSE.
                  MySQL internally stores this as TINYINT(1): 1 = TRUE, 0 = FALSE.

    Constraint  : DEFAULT TRUE
                  If no value is given during INSERT, the column automatically becomes TRUE.

    Use when    : you need a simple yes/no flag.
                  e.g., is_verified BOOL DEFAULT FALSE
                        is_deleted   BOOL DEFAULT FALSE

=======================================================
CONSTRAINTS
=======================================================

Constraints are rules applied to a column to control what data is allowed.

-------------------------------------------------------
1. AUTO_INCREMENT
-------------------------------------------------------

    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY

    What it does : MySQL automatically assigns the next available integer
                   when a new row is inserted. You don't provide this value manually.

    Rule         : Can only be used on INT (or its variants).
                   Only one AUTO_INCREMENT column allowed per table.

    Example behavior:
        INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Alice', 'alice@mail.com');
        -- id becomes 1 automatically

        INSERT INTO users (name, email) VALUES ('Bob', 'bob@mail.com');
        -- id becomes 2 automatically

-------------------------------------------------------
2. PRIMARY KEY
-------------------------------------------------------

    id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY

    What it does : Uniquely identifies each row in the table.
                   No two rows can have the same PRIMARY KEY value.
                   A PRIMARY KEY column cannot be NULL.

    Rule         : Each table should have exactly one PRIMARY KEY.
                   It can be a single column or a combination of columns (composite key).

    Why it matters: Other tables use this value to reference this row (via FOREIGN KEY).

-------------------------------------------------------
3. NOT NULL
-------------------------------------------------------

    name VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL

    What it does : Prevents the column from being left empty.
                   If you try to INSERT a row without providing this column, MySQL raises an error.

    Use when     : the data is mandatory.
                   e.g., a user must always have a name and email.

-------------------------------------------------------
4. UNIQUE
-------------------------------------------------------

    email VARCHAR(100) UNIQUE NOT NULL

    What it does : Ensures no two rows have the same value in this column.
                   Unlike PRIMARY KEY, a UNIQUE column can contain NULL
                   (but only one NULL per column in most databases).

    Difference from PRIMARY KEY:
        PRIMARY KEY -- unique + not null + only one per table
        UNIQUE      -- unique + allows null + can have multiple per table

-------------------------------------------------------
5. DEFAULT
-------------------------------------------------------

    is_active  BOOL      DEFAULT TRUE
    created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP

    What it does : Provides a fallback value when no value is given during INSERT.
                   MySQL uses the DEFAULT value automatically.

    Use when     : most rows will share a common starting value.
                   e.g., new users are active by default, created_at fills itself.

-------------------------------------------------------
6. FOREIGN KEY  (bonus -- commonly paired with PRIMARY KEY)
-------------------------------------------------------

    FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id)

    What it does : Links a column in one table to the PRIMARY KEY of another table.
                   Enforces referential integrity -- you cannot insert a value that
                   doesn't exist in the referenced table.

    Example:
        CREATE TABLE orders (
            order_id INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
            user_id  INT NOT NULL,
            amount   DECIMAL(10, 2),
            FOREIGN KEY (user_id) REFERENCES users(id)
        );
        -- user_id must match an existing id in the users table.

=======================================================
QUICK REFERENCE SUMMARY
=======================================================

Data Type     | Use For                          | Example
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
INT           | Whole numbers, IDs, counts       | id INT
VARCHAR(n)    | Variable text up to n chars      | name VARCHAR(100)
CHAR(n)       | Fixed-length text                | country_code CHAR(2)
ENUM(...)     | One value from a fixed list      | gender ENUM('M','F')
DATE          | Date only (YYYY-MM-DD)           | dob DATE
TIME          | Time only (HH:MM:SS)             | start_time TIME
DATETIME      | Date + time, no timezone         | event_at DATETIME
TIMESTAMP     | Date + time, timezone-aware      | created_at TIMESTAMP
BOOL          | True / False flags               | is_active BOOL
DECIMAL(p,s)  | Exact decimals (money etc.)      | price DECIMAL(10,2)
TEXT          | Long text (no length limit)      | bio TEXT

Constraint        | What it enforces
-------------------------------------------------------
AUTO_INCREMENT    | Auto-generates next integer value
PRIMARY KEY       | Unique + Not Null + one per table
NOT NULL          | Column must always have a value
UNIQUE            | No duplicate values in column
DEFAULT <value>   | Uses fallback if no value given
FOREIGN KEY       | Links to PRIMARY KEY in another table

